From 67e1532e202dbf953855ce4a147aed45452bc4f3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: sbihaiko <bihaiko@protonmail.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 10:31:08 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] nx2: say where the time of each long frame went, with
 EDEN_NX2_STALL_REPORT=<ms>

Ocarina of Time stops for half a second to four seconds now and then, and
no log line says on what. common/stall_ledger.h is one ledger for the
process: per category, relaxed counters of time, count and bytes, and the
longest piece. The sites that already log add what they spend to it:

  buffer cache    CreateBuffer (with its merge of overlaps), UploadMemory
  pipelines       built on the GPU thread (ReportRuntimePipeline), and the
                  GPU thread's waits for a worker's pipeline
  split GPU       the front's waits for the back: Drain with the list zone
                  (a command list on a GPU-written page), and every other
                  Drain, DrainTo and WaitForSpace (the ring full)
  texture cache   RunGarbageCollector, RefreshContents (bytes for a whole
                  image upload)
  fences          MasterSemaphore::Wait past its fast checks
  audio           a sink block padded with the held frame because the
                  queue was empty (SinkStream::held_frames counts the
                  frames for the output thread)

The frontend reads the ledger at every presented frame and prints one
EDEN_STALL line for a frame longer than <ms>, from the difference of two
readings rather than a clear the other threads race. Guest memory packing
and the staging buffers are timed in the fork itself.

Off by default; with the switch unset each site costs one check of a
cached flag, nothing is timed, and no output changes.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
